GUIDES = []
GUIDES.append(dict(slug="how-to-price-airbnb", icon="str", product="str", tool="airbnb-rental-profit-calculator", kicker="Short-term rental", mins=7,
 title="How to Price an Airbnb: Step-by-Step Rate Guide",
 h1="How to Price an Airbnb: A Step-by-Step Method",
 desc="How to set Airbnb nightly rates: find your cost floor, research comparable listings, adjust for seasons and lead time, and set minimum stays and fees.",
 dek="Start from your costs, check the market, then adjust for seasons, days of the week and how far ahead guests book.",
 body='''
<p>Pricing a short-term rental is not about guessing the "right" number once. It is a system: a floor you never go below, a base rate grounded in what similar places charge, and adjustments for the times when demand is higher or lower. This guide walks through that system step by step.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Know your floor</h2>
<p>Before you look at a single competitor, work out the lowest average rate at which the listing still covers its costs. Add up your fixed monthly costs (rent or mortgage, utilities, internet, insurance, software) and your per-stay costs (cleaning, laundry, supplies), and take platform fees into account. Our <a href="/tools/airbnb-rental-profit-calculator.html">short-term rental profit calculator</a> shows this as the <strong>break-even nightly rate</strong> for a given occupancy.</p>
<p>The floor is not your price. It is the line you only cross deliberately, for example to fill a gap night that would otherwise earn nothing.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Find real comparables</h2>
<p>Search the platform as a guest would, for your area and for specific future dates, and note listings that genuinely compete with yours: same type of place, similar number of guests and bedrooms, similar location and quality. Look at the total price a guest sees for a typical stay, including the cleaning fee, not only the headline nightly rate.</p>
<ul>
<li>Check several date ranges: a weekday in a quiet month, a weekend, and a known busy period.</li>
<li>Pay attention to which listings are already booked for near dates; booked calendars tell you more than prices alone.</li>
<li>Note the reviews and ratings: a new listing with no reviews usually needs to start below established neighbours.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Set a base rate</h2>
<p>Your base rate is the price for an ordinary night: not a holiday, not a big event, not the dead of the low season. Place it within the range of your comparables according to how your listing stacks up. If you are new, a slightly lower rate can help win the first bookings and reviews; raise it once reviews arrive.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Adjust for demand</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Seasons:</strong> raise prices in your high season and lower them in the low season. Your own booking history is the best guide once you have it.</li>
<li><strong>Days of the week:</strong> city apartments and leisure destinations often have different busy days. Price the busy ones higher.</li>
<li><strong>Events and holidays:</strong> concerts, conferences and public holidays can lift demand sharply for a few nights. Check local event calendars well ahead.</li>
<li><strong>Lead time:</strong> if a date is close and still empty, a modest discount may fill it. If dates far in the future book unusually fast, your price may be too low.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 5: Use minimum stays and fees on purpose</h2>
<p>Every stay means a turnover: cleaning, laundry and your time. A minimum stay of two or three nights reduces turnovers and can raise profit even if occupancy falls slightly. The cleaning fee also shapes demand: a high fee makes one-night stays look expensive, while folding part of the cleaning cost into the nightly rate makes short stays more competitive. Test both in the calculator to see the effect on monthly profit.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Review and repeat</h2>
<p>Set a regular time, for example every week, to look at the next 60 to 90 days. Are gaps forming? Are weekends booking months ahead? Adjust in small steps and note what happened. Dynamic pricing tools can automate much of this, but they still need a sensible base rate and floor from you.</p>
<p class="note-box">This guide is general information. Local rules, licences and taxes for short-term rentals vary widely; check them before you list.</p>
'''))
GUIDES.append(dict(slug="hyrox-pacing-strategy", icon="hyrox", product="hyrox", tool="hyrox-finish-time-calculator", kicker="Fitness · Hyrox", mins=6,
 title="Hyrox Pacing Strategy: How to Pace Runs and Stations",
 h1="Hyrox Pacing Strategy: Runs, Stations and Roxzone",
 desc="A practical Hyrox pacing strategy: why even pacing wins, how to run the first kilometres, sleds, lunges and wall balls, and race-day split sheets.",
 dek="Even pacing, controlled first kilometres and a plan for the sleds and wall balls: how to avoid the most common Hyrox mistake.",
 body='''
<p>Hyrox combines eight 1 km runs with eight workout stations, so it is closer to an endurance race than to a gym workout. The most common mistake is the same one runners make in a marathon: starting too fast. Because the stations fatigue the legs and grip, energy spent too early is paid back with interest later. A pacing plan helps you finish strong.</p>
<h2>Know the order</h2>
<p>The order never changes: SkiErg, Sled Push, Sled Pull, Burpee Broad Jumps, Rowing, Farmers Carry, Sandbag Lunges and Wall Balls, each preceded by a 1 km run. The second half is leg-heavy: lunges and wall balls come when you are most tired, so a plan that leaves something in the tank for the last two stations usually pays off.</p>
<h2>Pace the runs by effort, not ego</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Run 1</strong> feels easy because you are fresh and surrounded by other athletes. Hold back to your planned pace; it is the cheapest place to lose a race.</li>
<li><strong>Runs 2 to 4</strong> come after the SkiErg and the sleds. Expect your legs to feel heavy for the first few hundred metres and settle into rhythm rather than sprinting to recover lost time.</li>
<li><strong>Runs 5 to 8</strong> are where good pacing shows. If you held back early, you can keep a steady pace while others slow down.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many athletes find that their run splits get slower over the race even when their effort feels constant. The <a href="/tools/hyrox-finish-time-calculator.html">Hyrox finish time calculator</a> lets you model this with its run fade setting.</p>
<h2>Station strategy</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>SkiErg and Rowing:</strong> steady, repeatable strokes at a pace you could hold a little longer. Going all out on the erg spikes your heart rate before the next run.</li>
<li><strong>Sled Push and Pull:</strong> these punish poor technique more than lack of fitness. Low body position on the push, and a consistent hand-over-hand or walking pattern on the pull, keep you moving with fewer stops.</li>
<li><strong>Burpee Broad Jumps:</strong> find a rhythm you can keep without long pauses; many short rests cost more time than a slightly slower continuous pace.</li>
<li><strong>Farmers Carry:</strong> grip is the limiter. Fast, short steps and as few put-downs as possible.</li>
<li><strong>Sandbag Lunges and Wall Balls:</strong> plan your sets before the race (for example, wall balls in fixed sets with short planned breaks) so tired decisions are not made on the spot.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Do not ignore the Roxzone</h2>
<p>Transition time counts. Walk or jog purposefully through the Roxzone, know where each station is from the course map, and avoid long breaks before you start a station. Seconds saved in each of sixteen transitions add up.</p>
<h2>Use a split sheet</h2>
<p>Write your target elapsed time after each segment on a band or card. If you are well ahead early, ease off; if you are behind after the second half starts, do not panic, because most athletes slow down there too. Build your own sheet with the calculator by entering your training times; its split table gives the elapsed time after every run and station.</p>
<p class="note-box">General training information, not individual coaching or medical advice. Increase training load gradually and check the current Hyrox rulebook for your division's standards.</p>
'''))
GUIDES.append(dict(slug="debt-snowball-vs-avalanche", icon="debt", product="debt", tool="debt-payoff-calculator", kicker="Debt payoff", mins=6,
 title="Debt Snowball vs Avalanche: Which Is Better?",
 h1="Debt Snowball vs Avalanche, Explained",
 desc="Debt snowball vs avalanche with a worked example: how each orders your debts, which saves more interest, which keeps you motivated and how to pick.",
 dek="Two ways to order the same payments. One usually saves more interest; the other gives quicker wins. Here is how to choose.",
 body='''
<p>If you have several debts, such as credit cards, a car loan and a personal loan, you need a plan for which one to attack first. The two best-known plans are the <strong>debt snowball</strong> and the <strong>debt avalanche</strong>. They share the same engine and differ only in the order.</p>
<h2>The shared engine</h2>
<ol>
<li>List every debt with its balance, interest rate (APR) and minimum payment.</li>
<li>Decide on a fixed total monthly amount: all the minimums plus an extra amount you can keep paying.</li>
<li>Pay the minimum on every debt, and put all the extra on one target debt.</li>
<li>When the target is paid off, add its former payment to the next target. The payment "rolls" and grows.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Debt snowball: smallest balance first</h2>
<p>The snowball targets the debt with the smallest balance, regardless of interest rate. The first debt disappears quickly, which frees up its minimum payment and gives a visible win. For many people that early progress is what keeps them going for the months or years a payoff takes.</p>
<h2>Debt avalanche: highest interest rate first</h2>
<p>The avalanche targets the debt with the highest interest rate. Because the extra money always goes where interest is most expensive, it minimises total interest and generally finishes no later than the snowball. The downside: if the highest-rate debt also has a large balance, the first win can take a long time.</p>
<h2>A worked comparison</h2>
<p>Take four example debts: a $1,200 card at 19.9% APR (minimum $40), a $3,200 card at 24.9% ($95), a $4,000 personal loan at 12% ($130) and an $8,500 car loan at 7.5% ($260), with $200 a month on top of the minimums. Running them through our <a href="/tools/debt-payoff-calculator.html">snowball vs avalanche calculator</a> (monthly interest, payments rolled forward) gives:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Snowball</strong> clears the $1,200 card in month 6, the $3,200 card in month 16, the personal loan in month 21 and the car loan in month 27. Total interest: about $2,277.</li>
<li><strong>Avalanche</strong> clears the $3,200 card first, in month 13, then the $1,200 card in month 16, and finishes on the same schedule. Total interest: about $2,217.</li>
<li><strong>Minimums only</strong>, without rolling payments forward, takes almost five years and costs about $4,669 in interest.</li>
</ul>
<p>In this example the avalanche saves about $60 and both finish in the same month, while the snowball delivers its first paid-off debt seven months earlier. The real lesson is the third line: <em>either</em> method roughly halves the interest compared with paying minimums. With other numbers, especially a large balance at a high rate, the gap between the two methods can be much bigger, so test your own debts.</p>
<h2>How to choose</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Small interest difference?</strong> Pick the method you will stick with. For many people that is the snowball.</li>
<li><strong>Large difference</strong>, usually a big high-rate balance? The avalanche saves real money.</li>
<li><strong>Hybrid:</strong> clear one or two tiny balances for momentum, then switch to avalanche order.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Make it stick</h2>
<p>Automate the minimum payments so nothing is missed, schedule the extra payment for payday, and track progress monthly. Avoid adding new debt to the cards you are paying off. If a minimum payment does not cover the monthly interest, that debt will never shrink on its own; contact the lender or a non-profit credit counsellor about options.</p>
<p class="note-box">Educational content, not personal financial advice. Lenders may calculate interest differently (for example daily) and apply fees, so treat any calculation as an estimate.</p>
'''))
GUIDES.append(dict(slug="position-sizing-explained", icon="risk", product="trading", tool="position-size-calculator", kicker="Forex · Gold", mins=6,
 title="Position Sizing Explained: Risk per Trade, Lots, Pips",
 h1="How Position Sizing Works in Forex and Gold",
 desc="Position sizing explained: fixed-percentage risk, stop-loss distance, pip value and lot size, with examples for EUR/USD, USD/JPY and XAU/USD.",
 dek="Risk a planned amount, let the stop-loss set the distance, and let the size follow. The maths behind every trade size.",
 body='''
<p class="note-box"><strong>Educational content only.</strong> Leveraged trading in forex and gold carries a high risk of loss. Nothing here is a recommendation to trade.</p>
<p>Two traders can take the same trade, at the same price, with the same stop, and have completely different outcomes, because one traded ten times the size. Position sizing is the part of trading that decides how much a loss actually costs. It is also the part you fully control.</p>
<h2>The three inputs</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Money at risk:</strong> how much of the account you accept to lose if the trade fails. A common approach is a fixed percentage of the current balance.</li>
<li><strong>Stop-loss distance:</strong> how far the price must move against you, in pips or price units, before the trade idea is wrong. It comes from the chart, not from the account size.</li>
<li><strong>Value per pip:</strong> how much one pip is worth for one lot of the instrument.</li>
</ol>
<p>Put together: <strong>lots = money at risk ÷ (stop distance in pips × pip value per lot)</strong>.</p>
<h2>Example: EUR/USD</h2>
<p>A $5,000 account risking 1% has $50 at risk. The stop is 25 pips away. On a standard lot (100,000 euros) one pip, 0.0001, is worth $10, so 25 pips cost $250 per lot. $50 ÷ $250 = <strong>0.20 lots</strong>, or two mini lots.</p>
<h2>Example: USD/JPY</h2>
<p>For USD/JPY one pip is 0.01 yen, so a standard lot of $100,000 moves 1,000 yen per pip. To convert to dollars, divide by the exchange rate: at 150.00, 1,000 ÷ 150 ≈ $6.67 per pip. With the same $50 risk and a 25-pip stop: $50 ÷ (25 × $6.67) ≈ <strong>0.30 lots</strong>. The pip value changes as the rate moves, so recalculate rather than reuse an old number.</p>
<h2>Example: XAU/USD</h2>
<p>Gold contracts are commonly 100 ounces per lot, so a $1 move in gold is $100 per lot. Brokers define the "pip" differently ($0.01 or $0.10), which is a frequent source of confusion. Think in dollars of price movement instead: a $5 stop on one lot is $500. With $50 at risk, $50 ÷ $500 = <strong>0.10 lots</strong>. Always confirm the contract size in your platform's specification.</p>
<h2>Why a fixed percentage?</h2>
<p>Risking a fixed percentage of the current balance means positions shrink automatically after losses and grow after gains. It also makes losing streaks survivable: every trader has them, and the size of each loss determines how deep the drawdown gets. Choosing the percentage is a personal decision; the point is to choose it before the trade, not during it.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Moving the stop to fit a size.</strong> The stop belongs where the idea is invalid; adjust the size instead.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring the pip value.</strong> The same lot size risks different amounts on EUR/USD, USD/JPY and gold.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting costs.</strong> Spreads, commissions and slippage add to every loss, especially with tight stops.</li>
<li><strong>Not recording trades.</strong> A journal with planned risk and actual result shows whether your sizing rules are being followed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <a href="/tools/position-size-calculator.html">position size calculator</a> does this maths for EUR/USD-style pairs, USD/JPY, USD/CHF, USD/CAD and gold, and rounds down so you never risk more than planned.</p>
'''))

GUIDES.append(dict(slug="how-to-calculate-airbnb-occupancy-rate", icon="str", product="str", tool="airbnb-rental-profit-calculator", kicker="Short-term rental", mins=6,
 title="Airbnb Occupancy Rate: How to Calculate It (+ ADR)",
 h1="How to Calculate Airbnb Occupancy Rate, ADR and RevPAR",
 desc="How to calculate Airbnb occupancy rate, average daily rate (ADR), RevPAR and break-even occupancy, with formulas and a worked example for hosts.",
 dek="Four numbers tell you how a short-term rental is really doing. Here is how to calculate each one and what to do with them.",
 body='''
<p>Hosts often judge a listing by how full the calendar looks. A full calendar at a low price can earn less than a half-empty one at a good price, so it helps to track a few standard numbers instead. This guide shows the formulas, a worked example and how the numbers fit together.</p>
<h2>Occupancy rate</h2>
<p><strong>Occupancy rate = booked nights ÷ available nights × 100%.</strong></p>
<p>Count only nights the place was actually available to guests. Nights you blocked for yourself, for repairs or because of a minimum-stay rule you chose are usually left out of "available". Use the same rule every month, otherwise the trend means nothing.</p>
<p>Example: in a 30-night month you blocked 2 nights for maintenance, so 28 nights were available. Guests booked 19 of them. Occupancy is 19 ÷ 28 = <strong>67.9%</strong>.</p>
<h2>Average daily rate (ADR)</h2>
<p><strong>ADR = room revenue ÷ booked nights.</strong></p>
<p>Room revenue here means what guests paid for the nights themselves. Many hosts keep cleaning fees out of ADR and track them separately, because they cover a cost rather than the stay. Decide once and stay consistent.</p>
<p>Example: the 19 booked nights brought in $2,280 in nightly revenue. ADR is $2,280 ÷ 19 = <strong>$120</strong>.</p>
<h2>RevPAR: revenue per available night</h2>
<p><strong>RevPAR = room revenue ÷ available nights</strong>, which is the same as <strong>ADR × occupancy rate</strong>.</p>
<p>Example: $2,280 ÷ 28 = <strong>$81.43</strong> (or $120 × 67.9%). RevPAR is the most useful single number for pricing decisions, because it rewards the balance between price and occupancy. Raising the rate is only a win if RevPAR goes up too.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Booked nights</th><th>ADR</th><th>Occupancy</th><th>RevPAR</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Lower price</td><td>24 of 28</td><td>$100</td><td>85.7%</td><td>$85.71</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example month</td><td>19 of 28</td><td>$120</td><td>67.9%</td><td>$81.43</td></tr>
<tr><td>Higher price</td><td>15 of 28</td><td>$145</td><td>53.6%</td><td>$77.68</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>In this illustration the cheaper month earns the most per available night, but it also means 24 turnovers' worth of guests, against 15 at the higher price. That is why profit, not only revenue, decides which option is better.</p>
<h2>Break-even occupancy</h2>
<p>Break-even occupancy is the share of nights you must book just to cover your costs. Roughly:</p>
<p><strong>Break-even occupancy = fixed monthly costs ÷ (available nights × (ADR − variable cost per night))</strong></p>
<p>Variable cost per night includes platform fees and your cleaning and supplies spread over the nights of an average stay. Example: fixed costs of $1,400 a month, ADR $120, platform fee 15% ($18) and $22 of cleaning and supplies per night gives $80 left per booked night. $1,400 ÷ (28 × $80) = <strong>62.5%</strong>. Below that occupancy the month loses money at this price.</p>
<p>The <a href="/tools/airbnb-rental-profit-calculator.html">Airbnb profit and occupancy calculator</a> does this with your own numbers, including cleaning fees per stay and the break-even nightly rate.</p>
<h2>How to use these numbers</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Track monthly and compare the same month year on year.</strong> Seasonality makes month-to-month comparisons misleading.</li>
<li><strong>Watch RevPAR when you change prices.</strong> If occupancy drops but RevPAR rises, the change probably worked; check profit too.</li>
<li><strong>Keep break-even occupancy well below your typical occupancy.</strong> The gap is your safety margin for quiet months.</li>
<li><strong>Use your own data first.</strong> Market reports are useful context, but your listing's history is the best guide. See <a href="/guides/how-to-price-airbnb.html">how to price an Airbnb</a> for setting the rates themselves.</li>
</ul>
<p class="note-box">The figures above are a worked illustration, not market averages. Local rules, licences and taxes for short-term rentals vary; check them before you list.</p>
'''))
